## Which to choose?

| | Firebase | Supabase |
|---|---|---|
| Data model | Document/NoSQL (Firestore) | Relational (Postgres) |
| Built-in TTL | Yes (native Firestore TTL) | No — use `pg_cron` |
| Realtime | `onSnapshot` listeners | `postgres_changes` channels |
| Auth | Phone auth via Firebase Auth | Phone auth via Supabase Auth (needs SMS provider) |
| Row-level security | Firestore Security Rules | Postgres RLS policies (shown above) |
| Good fit if | You want the least backend code to maintain | You want SQL, joins, and full Postgres tooling |

Either backend slots into the existing PWA: replace the in-memory `state.contacts` / `state.chats` / `state.messages` objects in `index.html` with reads from Firestore or Supabase, and call `sendMessage()` from above instead of the local-only version currently in the file.

## Capacitor notes (for the native conversion)

- Use `@capacitor-community/contacts` to read the device address book and match phone numbers against your `users`/`profiles` table — this is what should ultimately populate "Sync phone contacts" in the app instead of the manual "New Contact" form.
- Both Firebase and Supabase JS SDKs work unchanged inside a Capacitor WebView; no native plugin is required for auth/data, only for contacts, push notifications, and camera/microphone access.